        "descriptions": [
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "With the rise of the Internet and social media platforms, people now have access to more weather information than ever before. Although this allows end users to receive weather information from a variety of sources, the sheer volume of available information likely contributes to a perception that weather risk messages are inconsistent. Members of the weather enterprise share these concerns and believe that these perceived inconsistencies may negatively affect their many audiences. The challenge, however, is that there are only a few studies to-date that have explored message consistency in a weather context. Therefore, in its current state, the weather enterprise lacks empirical evidence that (1) demonstrates that message consistency is a relevant operational concern and (2) provides research-guided recommendations to practitioners and operational meteorologists on how to achieve a more consistent message. To address this operational need, this research project conceptualized ‘message consistency’ for the weather enterprise and employs a social science mixed-methods approach to explore the effects of weather-related graphical inconsistencies on lay public end users. In particular, this project used the Storm Prediction Center’s (SPC) Convective Outlook graphic as a vehicle to investigate the role that visual design plays in keeping a weather-related message consistent.",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          },
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "Whereas the previous mission asked participants to evaluate risk perception, uncertainty, and behavioral intentions at one time point (i.e., after seeing two Convective Outlook graphics), Study 5 used a repeated-measures design. Inspired by a study by Demuth et al. (2023), a repeated-measures design is a research design that involves taking multiple measures of the same perceptual variables over two or more time periods. As a result, our study design involved measuring risk perception, uncertainty, and behavioral intentions at two time-points: (1) After seeing a single Convective Outlook graphic and (2) After seeing two Convective Outlook graphics side-by-side. The goal of this experimental design was to more clearly understand the effects of inconsistent Convective Outlook graphics by being able to measure the change in risk perception, uncertainty, and behavioral intentions between the two time-points.\n\nAfter the fourth mission determined that a majority of the significant relationships with our perceptual variables occurred at the risk gap extremes (i.e., +1 risk category and -1 risk category), only those risk gap conditions were kept for Study 5. Finally, because this study was designed to more clearly understand the effects of inconsistent color schemes, a low saturation color scheme was used. During the first mission, participants frequently mentioned that more saturated colors communicated a more severe message. Therefore, we used this opportunity to explore this finding among a larger, more representative sample of the public. This resulted in this using a 3 (risk gap) x 2 (color scheme) single factor, randomized experimental design. This means there was a total of 5 experimental conditions.\n\nThe survey began with a few survey items asking participants to provide their perceived risk and behavioral intentions to\nsevere weather forecasts that used various colors to depict the same severe weather potential. Like the analysis performed on various risk words and phrases (see Williams et al. 2022), this item-set was developed to provide a bit more insight around the effects of color on people’s risk perception and behavioral intentions. \n\nLike the previous missions in this project, the survey instrument used a hypothetical scenario that informed participants that meteorologists were forecasting a threat of severe weather for their area on a Saturday afternoon. After reading the scenario, participants were randomly assigned to one of the 5 experimental conditions. Like the fourth mission, this study also used a tiered approach to show people the first graphic (manipulated based on their experimental condition) and the hypothetical scenario above. Then, after 18 seconds had passed, participants were given the option to continue to the next survey page. However, instead of showing participants a second graphic immediately, this study asked participants to complete survey items that assessed perceived risk, uncertainty, and behavioral intentions after seeing only that first\ngraphic. After completing those survey items, participants were then told that they decided to search for more information and shown the same graphic they saw first. After 9 seconds, a second graphic from another source appeared on the screen next to the first graphic. Participants were then asked to evaluate perceived consistency and complete the same survey\nitems after seeing both graphics. This new methodology was used to collect baseline information after seeing a single Convective Outlook graphic to better quantify the effects of an (in)consistent graphic on perceived risk, uncertainty, and behavioral intentions.",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          }
        ],
